            <ArticleTitle>Left internal thoracic artery and saphenous vein as a composite graft: 8-year angiographic follow-up study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>118-21</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0102-76382010000100024</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The use of a composite graft with left internal thoracic artery (LITA) and arterial or saphenous vein (SV) grafts can allow the complete revascularization of the left coronary system (LCS) without cardiopulmonary bypass (CPB) and without ascending aorta manipulation (AAM), in order to reduce some complications in the immediate postoperative. This study shows 8-year angiographic follow-up results of two patients underwent no-touch aorta off-pump coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) using LITA and SV as a composite graft to supply LCS.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
